; pi4NetProbe.pi4 - RaspberryPi4/Lib/net.pi4 walked from "does the
; protocol layer work with no wire at all" through to a board that
; answers `ping` from a PC, with every stage printing its reading before
; the next stage depends on it.
;
; DIAGNOSTIC. Does not ship.
;
;   PureMetalForge.exe --compile RaspberryPi4/Examples/Diagnostics/pi4NetProbe.pi4 -t pi4 \
;            --load-addr 0x400000 --stack-addr 0x3000000 \
;            --entry-returns -o net.img
;
; --entry-returns IS NOT OPTIONAL. Without it the payload runs off its
; own end and the board hangs. Twenty-one of the diagnostics in this
; directory need it and every one of them says so in its own header,
; because the build line is the thing people copy from the wrong file.
;
; ARM THE DEADMAN BEFORE RUNNING THIS: `deadman 45`. Stage 6 listens for
; thirty seconds by design and a board that looks hung for thirty
; seconds is a board somebody power-cycles at second twenty-five.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  WHAT THIS IS PROVING, AND IN WHAT ORDER
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  net.pi4 sits on top of genet.pi4 and does no input or output of its
;  own - see the long note in its header. So this probe is the pump:
;  GenetRecvWait() gets a frame, NetInput() decides what it is, and if
;  NetOutLen() is non-zero GenetSend() puts the answer back on the wire.
;  Nothing else is happening here.
;
;  1. GENET, briefly. Identity, PHY, link, bring-up. This probe does
;     NOT re-do pi4GenetProbe's work - that file is the place to go when
;     the wire is the problem - but nothing above layer 2 means anything
;     until layer 2 is up, so the readings are printed and the run stops
;     if they are bad.
;
;  2. THE PROTOCOL LAYER, OFF THE WIRE. This is the stage that keeps its
;     value forever and it is deliberately placed BEFORE anything
;     depends on the network. Two frames are held in this file as
;     literal bytes - a real ARP request and a real ICMP echo request -
;     and they are fed straight into NetInput(). If the replies come out
;     right, the whole of ARP, IPv4, the checksums and ICMP are proven
;     WITHOUT A CABLE, A SWITCH OR A PC. If they come out wrong, nothing
;     that follows is worth reading and the cause is in this tree rather
;     than out on the bench.
;
;     THE TWO CANNED FRAMES ARE ADDRESSED TO 192.168.1.50 and this stage
;     therefore configures that address temporarily, prints that it is
;     doing so, and puts the real configuration back afterwards. The
;     alternative - patching the addresses in - would need the header
;     checksum recomputing, and recomputing it with the library's own
;     checksum is the library marking its own homework.
;
;  3. THE REAL CONFIGURATION, printed as dotted quads so a human can
;     compare it against what they typed on the PC.
;
;  4. ARP FOR THE GATEWAY. The first thing that requires the network to
;     answer. A request goes out and the receive loop runs until either
;     the reply arrives or the deadline passes; either way the result is
;     printed with the gateway's hardware address if we got one.
;
;  5. AN OUTBOUND PING. Four echo requests to #NP_PEER, matched by
;     identifier and sequence. This is the direction that needs ARP to
;     have worked, which is why it comes after stage 4.
;
;  6. THE SERVICE LOOP - THIRTY SECONDS OF ANSWERING. This is the stage
;     a `ping 192.168.1.50` is typed into. Every frame that arrives is
;     handed to NetInput() and every reply it stages is sent. A tally is
;     printed at the end: frames in, replies out, ARP answered, pings
;     answered, UDP received.
;
;     IT IS A DURATION AND NOT A POLL COUNT. Sixty passes of a 500 ms
;     GenetRecvWait() is thirty seconds of real listening. A bare loop
;     of GenetRecv() calls is microseconds, and on 2026-08-26 exactly
;     that mistake produced a "receive is broken" report from a receive
;     path that was working perfectly - the whole story is in
;     GenetRecvWait's header in genet.pi4.
;
;  7. UDP. Whatever arrived on #NP_UDP_PORT during stage 6 is reported,
;     and one datagram is sent to the peer.
;
;  8. THE REFUSALS, every one of them provoked deliberately and printed
;     with the code it should have returned. THIS IS THE HALF OF THE
;     PROBE THAT KEEPS ITS VALUE FOREVER: a refusal that stops firing is
;     a refusal somebody deleted, and this is where that shows up.
;     IT RUNS LAST AND IT LEAVES THE STACK UNCONFIGURED, because
;     provoking #NET_E_NO_MAC means calling NetReset(). Nothing after it
;     would work and nothing is put after it.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  WHAT TO TYPE ON THE PC, AND WHAT EACH OUTCOME MEANS
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Put the PC on the same subnet as #NP_IP below, then during stage 6:
;
;      ping 192.168.1.50
;
;  Reply from 192.168.1.50: bytes=32 time<1ms TTL=64
;      Everything works. TTL=64 is ours (#NET_IP_TTL_DEFAULT); a TTL
;      that is not 64 or 63 means something else answered.
;
;  Request timed out.
;      Something between the ARP and the echo failed. Read the tally at
;      the end of stage 6: "arp answered 0" means the PC's ARP never
;      reached us or our reply never reached it, which is layer 2;
;      "arp answered 1, ping answered 0" means ARP works and ICMP does
;      not, which is this file's problem.
;
;  Destination host unreachable.
;      The PC could not even ARP for us - wrong subnet on one side, or
;      the board never sent an ARP reply. Check `arp -a` on the PC.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  UNITS
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Lengths in bytes, times in milliseconds, addresses printed as dotted
;  quads. Speeds in Mbit/s, which is what stage 1 borrows from
;  pi4GenetProbe.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

XIncludeFile "Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/uart.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/genet.pi4"
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Network/net.pbi"

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE THINGS TO CHANGE BEFORE A RUN
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; The board's own address, its netmask, its gateway, and one peer to
; ping outbound. All four as four octets, so nothing here depends on
; knowing which end of a 32-bit number the first octet lives at.
#NP_IP_A = 169
#NP_IP_B = 254
#NP_IP_C = 183
#NP_IP_D = 50

#NP_MASK_A = 255
#NP_MASK_B = 255
#NP_MASK_C = 0
#NP_MASK_D = 0

#NP_GW_A = 169
#NP_GW_B = 254
#NP_GW_C = 183
#NP_GW_D = 67

; Who to ping outbound in stage 5, and who to send a datagram to in
; stage 7. The gateway is the safest default because it is the one
; machine on the segment that is certain to exist and certain to answer
; ARP.
#NP_PEER_A = 169
#NP_PEER_B = 254
#NP_PEER_C = 183
#NP_PEER_D = 67

#NP_UDP_PORT = 9000        ; what we bind, and where stage 7 sends

; The RX region genet.pi4 requires. 512 KiB - 256 descriptors of 2048
; bytes - at $04000000, in the low DRAM bank, well clear of the image at
; $00400000 and the stack top at $03000000.
#NP_RX_REGION = $04000000
#NP_RX_BYTES  = 524288

#NP_LINK_MS = 2000         ; how long to wait for auto-negotiation
#NP_TX_MS   = 100          ; how long to wait for one frame to leave

; Stage 4 and 5: how long to wait for the network to answer one
; question, and in how many slices. 8 x 250 ms = two seconds.
#NP_ANSWER_SLICE_MS = 250
#NP_ANSWER_SLICES   = 8

; Stage 6: 60 x 500 ms = thirty seconds. A DURATION, NOT A POLL COUNT -
; see the header.
#NP_SERVE_SLICE_MS = 500
#NP_SERVE_SLICES   = 60

Global Dim npMac.a[6]
Global Dim npRx.a[1600]
Global Dim npPeerMac.a[6]
Global Dim npArp.a[60]         ; a real ARP request, byte for byte
Global Dim npEcho.a[74]        ; a real ICMP echo request, byte for byte
Global Dim npUdpOut.a[64]

Global npIp.i
Global npMask.i
Global npGw.i
Global npPeer.i

; Stage 6's tally.
Global npFramesIn.i
Global npRepliesOut.i
Global npArpAnswered.i
Global npPingAnswered.i
Global npUdpIn.i
Global npSendFail.i


Procedure PutHexN(v.i, digits.i)
  Define i.i
  Define n.i
  For i = digits - 1 To 0 Step -1
    n = (v >> (i * 4)) & $F
    If n < 10
      UartWrite(48 + n)
    Else
      UartWrite(55 + n)
    EndIf
  Next
EndProcedure

Procedure Line(*name, v.i)
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  UartWriteStr(*name)
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  PrintDec(v)
  PrintNl()
EndProcedure

Procedure HexLine(*name, v.i, digits.i)
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  UartWriteStr(*name)
  UartWriteStr("  $")
  PutHexN(v, digits)
  PrintNl()
EndProcedure

; An IPv4 address the way a person writes one. net.pi4 holds an address
; with its FIRST OCTET IN THE MOST SIGNIFICANT BYTE, so this reads it
; from the top down and there is nothing to reverse.
Procedure DotLine(*name, ip.i)
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  UartWriteStr(*name)
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  PrintDec((ip >> 24) & $FF)
  UartWrite(46)                    ; ASCII full stop
  PrintDec((ip >> 16) & $FF)
  UartWrite(46)
  PrintDec((ip >> 8) & $FF)
  UartWrite(46)
  PrintDec(ip & $FF)
  PrintNl()
EndProcedure

Procedure MacLine(*name, *mac)
  Define i.i
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  UartWriteStr(*name)
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  For i = 0 To 5
    If i > 0
      UartWrite(58)                ; ASCII colon
    EndIf
    PutHexN(PeekA(*mac + i), 2)
  Next
  PrintNl()
EndProcedure

Procedure Say(*s)
  UartWriteStr(*s)
  PrintNl()
EndProcedure

; The last net.pi4 refusal, in words.
Procedure WhyNet()
  UartWriteStr("  -> ")
  UartWriteStr(NetErrorText())
  PrintNl()
EndProcedure

; The last genet.pi4 refusal, in words. Kept separate because the two
; libraries have separate error variables and printing the wrong one
; while chasing a fault costs more time than the two procedures cost.
Procedure WhyGenet()
  UartWriteStr("  -> ")
  UartWriteStr(GenetErrorText())
  PrintNl()
EndProcedure

; A refusal, provoked and graded on the spot. `got` is what came back
; and `want` is what the library documents. Printing both means a
; changed code shows up as a changed transcript rather than as silence.
Procedure Refusal(*name, got.i, want.i)
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  UartWriteStr(*name)
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  PrintDec(got)
  UartWriteStr("  (want ")
  PrintDec(want)
  UartWriteStr(")")
  If got <> want
    UartWriteStr("   *** WRONG ***")
  EndIf
  PrintNl()
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE TWO CANNED FRAMES
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Byte for byte what a Windows host puts on the wire. They are written
;  out longhand rather than generated, because a frame generated by the
;  library under test proves only that the library agrees with itself.
;
;  npArp - 60 octets. An ARP request, broadcast, from
;  dc:a6:32:11:22:33 at 192.168.1.20, asking who has 192.168.1.50.
;  42 octets of protocol and 18 of zero pad.
;
;  npEcho - 74 octets. An ICMP echo request to 192.168.1.50 from
;  192.168.1.20, identifier 1, sequence 37, carrying the 32-byte
;  lower-case a..w payload Microsoft's ping sends. Its IPv4 header
;  checksum ($A4F6) and its ICMP checksum ($4D36) are both correct, so
;  a library that fails to verify them and a library that verifies them
;  wrongly are BOTH caught here.
;
;  These are the same two frames tools/a64/a64_net_check.py feeds in, so
;  a disagreement between the board and the gate is a disagreement about
;  the same bytes.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure NpLoadFrames()
  npArp[0] = $FF : npArp[1] = $FF : npArp[2] = $FF : npArp[3] = $FF
  npArp[4] = $FF : npArp[5] = $FF                                  ; broadcast
  npArp[6] = $DC : npArp[7] = $A6 : npArp[8] = $32
  npArp[9] = $11 : npArp[10] = $22 : npArp[11] = $33               ; the asker
  npArp[12] = $08 : npArp[13] = $06                                ; ARP
  npArp[14] = $00 : npArp[15] = $01                                ; Ethernet
  npArp[16] = $08 : npArp[17] = $00                                ; IPv4
  npArp[18] = $06 : npArp[19] = $04                                ; 6 and 4
  npArp[20] = $00 : npArp[21] = $01                                ; request
  npArp[22] = $DC : npArp[23] = $A6 : npArp[24] = $32
  npArp[25] = $11 : npArp[26] = $22 : npArp[27] = $33              ; sender hw
  npArp[28] = $C0 : npArp[29] = $A8
  npArp[30] = $01 : npArp[31] = $14                                ; 192.168.1.20
  npArp[32] = $00 : npArp[33] = $00 : npArp[34] = $00
  npArp[35] = $00 : npArp[36] = $00 : npArp[37] = $00              ; target hw
  npArp[38] = $C0 : npArp[39] = $A8
  npArp[40] = $01 : npArp[41] = $32                                ; 192.168.1.50
  npArp[42] = $00 : npArp[43] = $00 : npArp[44] = $00 : npArp[45] = $00
  npArp[46] = $00 : npArp[47] = $00 : npArp[48] = $00 : npArp[49] = $00
  npArp[50] = $00 : npArp[51] = $00 : npArp[52] = $00 : npArp[53] = $00
  npArp[54] = $00 : npArp[55] = $00 : npArp[56] = $00 : npArp[57] = $00
  npArp[58] = $00 : npArp[59] = $00                                ; the pad

  npEcho[0] = $02 : npEcho[1] = $00 : npEcho[2] = $4D
  npEcho[3] = $46 : npEcho[4] = $00 : npEcho[5] = $01              ; to us
  npEcho[6] = $DC : npEcho[7] = $A6 : npEcho[8] = $32
  npEcho[9] = $11 : npEcho[10] = $22 : npEcho[11] = $33            ; from the PC
  npEcho[12] = $08 : npEcho[13] = $00                              ; IPv4
  npEcho[14] = $45 : npEcho[15] = $00                              ; version 4, IHL 5
  npEcho[16] = $00 : npEcho[17] = $3C                              ; total 60
  npEcho[18] = $12 : npEcho[19] = $34                              ; identification
  npEcho[20] = $40 : npEcho[21] = $00                              ; Don't Fragment
  npEcho[22] = $40                                                 ; TTL 64
  npEcho[23] = $01                                                 ; protocol ICMP
  npEcho[24] = $A4 : npEcho[25] = $F6                              ; header checksum
  npEcho[26] = $C0 : npEcho[27] = $A8
  npEcho[28] = $01 : npEcho[29] = $14                              ; from 192.168.1.20
  npEcho[30] = $C0 : npEcho[31] = $A8
  npEcho[32] = $01 : npEcho[33] = $32                              ; to 192.168.1.50
  npEcho[34] = $08 : npEcho[35] = $00                              ; echo request
  npEcho[36] = $4D : npEcho[37] = $36                              ; ICMP checksum
  npEcho[38] = $00 : npEcho[39] = $01                              ; identifier 1
  npEcho[40] = $00 : npEcho[41] = $25                              ; sequence 37
  npEcho[42] = $61 : npEcho[43] = $62 : npEcho[44] = $63 : npEcho[45] = $64
  npEcho[46] = $65 : npEcho[47] = $66 : npEcho[48] = $67 : npEcho[49] = $68
  npEcho[50] = $69 : npEcho[51] = $6A : npEcho[52] = $6B : npEcho[53] = $6C
  npEcho[54] = $6D : npEcho[55] = $6E : npEcho[56] = $6F : npEcho[57] = $70
  npEcho[58] = $71 : npEcho[59] = $72 : npEcho[60] = $73 : npEcho[61] = $74
  npEcho[62] = $75 : npEcho[63] = $76 : npEcho[64] = $77 : npEcho[65] = $61
  npEcho[66] = $62 : npEcho[67] = $63 : npEcho[68] = $64 : npEcho[69] = $65
  npEcho[70] = $66 : npEcho[71] = $67 : npEcho[72] = $68 : npEcho[73] = $69
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  ONE TURN OF THE PUMP.
;
;  Listen for `ms` milliseconds, hand anything that arrives to
;  NetInput(), send anything it stages, and return what NetInput()
;  returned. 0 means the deadline passed with nothing to do.
;
;  THE TALLY IS KEPT HERE so that every caller counts the same way.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i NpPump(ms.i)
  Define n.i
  Define r.i
  n = GenetRecvWait(@npRx[0], 1600, ms)
  If n <= 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  npFramesIn = npFramesIn + 1
  r = NetInput(#HW_LINK_WIRED, @npRx[0], n)
  If NetOutLen() > 0
    If GenetSend(NetOutBuf(), NetOutLen(), #NP_TX_MS) = 1
      npRepliesOut = npRepliesOut + 1
      If NetLastProto() = $0806
        npArpAnswered = npArpAnswered + 1
      Else
        npPingAnswered = npPingAnswered + 1
      EndIf
    Else
      npSendFail = npSendFail + 1
    EndIf
  EndIf
  If r = 2
    npUdpIn = npUdpIn + 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn r
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE CANNED SUBNET
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  npArp and npEcho are addressed to 192.168.1.50 byte by byte, so they
;  only mean anything against a stack configured as 192.168.1.50/24.
;  Stage 2 and stage 8 both replay them and both must configure it.
;
;  IT IS A PROCEDURE AND NOT A COMMENT because on 2026-08-26 stage 8
;  replayed them against the REAL bench address instead and the frames
;  were dropped, unremarked, by net.pi4's destination filter. A rule
;  written in a comment is a rule one of its two callers will forget.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i NpCannedConfig()
  ProcedureReturn NetSetIPv4(#HW_LINK_WIRED, NetMakeIPv4(192, 168, 1, 50), NetMakeIPv4(255, 255, 255, 0), 0)
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  RESOLVE THE PEER, AND ANSWER THE QUESTION THE BUILDER WILL ASK
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  1 if the peer's hardware address is known RIGHT NOW, having sent an
;  ARP request and pumped for it if it was not.
;
;  WHY THIS EXISTS. Stage 7 used to print "peer resolved" from one
;  NetArpLookup() near the top and then let NetPingBuild() ask the cache
;  again, seconds and several received frames later. On 2026-08-26 the
;  two answers differed and the stage printed both:
;
;      peer resolved  1
;      ping build     0   -> the hardware address is not known
;
;  A reader should not be asked to notice that two adjacent lines cannot
;  both be true. Every claim about resolution now comes from THIS
;  procedure, called immediately before the build that depends on it,
;  so there is only ever one answer in flight. The underlying cause -
;  an entry expiring mid-conversation - is fixed in net.pi4; see
;  net_ConfirmNeighbour there. This is the second half of that repair
;  and it is worth keeping even so, because an instrument that can
;  print a contradiction will eventually print one for a new reason.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i NpEnsurePeer()
  Define i.i
  Define r.i
  If NetArpLookup(#HW_LINK_WIRED, npPeer, @npPeerMac[0]) = 1
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  r = NetArpRequest(#HW_LINK_WIRED, npPeer)
  If r = 1
    GenetSend(NetOutBuf(), NetOutLen(), #NP_TX_MS)
  EndIf
  i = 0
  While i < #NP_ANSWER_SLICES
    NpPump(#NP_ANSWER_SLICE_MS)
    If NetArpLookup(#HW_LINK_WIRED, npPeer, @npPeerMac[0]) = 1
      ProcedureReturn 1
    EndIf
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure


Procedure.i Main()
  Define i.i
  Define n.i
  Define r.i
  Define ok.i
  Define phy.i
  Define sent.i
  Define got.i

  PrintNl()
  Say("=== net.pi4 - ARP, IPv4, ICMP and UDP over GENET ===")
  PrintNl()

  npMac[0] = $02
  npMac[1] = $00
  npMac[2] = $4D
  npMac[3] = $46
  npMac[4] = $00
  npMac[5] = $01
  NpLoadFrames()
  NetInit()

  npIp   = NetMakeIPv4(#NP_IP_A, #NP_IP_B, #NP_IP_C, #NP_IP_D)
  npMask = NetMakeIPv4(#NP_MASK_A, #NP_MASK_B, #NP_MASK_C, #NP_MASK_D)
  npGw   = NetMakeIPv4(#NP_GW_A, #NP_GW_B, #NP_GW_C, #NP_GW_D)
  npPeer = NetMakeIPv4(#NP_PEER_A, #NP_PEER_B, #NP_PEER_C, #NP_PEER_D)

  ; ==================================================================
  ;  1. GENET, BRIEFLY
  ; ==================================================================
  ; pi4GenetProbe.pi4 is the file for diagnosing layer 2. These are the
  ; four readings without which nothing above them means anything.
  Say("1. the wire")
  ok = GenetProbe()
  HexLine("SYS_REV_CTRL   ", GenetRevision(), 8)
  Line("probe ok       ", ok)
  If ok = 0
    WhyGenet()
    Say("=== stopping: the GENET block did not answer. Run pi4GenetProbe ===")
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  phy = GenetPhyScan()
  Line("PHY address    ", phy)
  If phy < 0
    WhyGenet()
    Say("=== stopping: no PHY. Run pi4GenetProbe ===")
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  HexLine("PHY id         ", GenetPhyId(), 8)

  ok = GenetPhyWaitLink(#NP_LINK_MS)
  Line("link           ", ok)
  If ok = 0
    WhyGenet()
    Say("  (no cable, or nothing on the other end)")
  EndIf
  If GenetPhyResolve() = 1
    Line("speed Mbit/s   ", GenetSpeed())
    Line("full duplex    ", GenetFullDuplex())
  EndIf
  PrintNl()

  ; ==================================================================
  ;  2. THE PROTOCOL LAYER, WITH NO WIRE INVOLVED
  ; ==================================================================
  ; The stage that keeps its value forever, and the one that says
  ; whether a later failure is out on the bench or in this tree. Two
  ; real frames go in and two real replies must come out, and neither
  ; the MAC nor the cable nor the switch is in the path.
  Say("2. protocol self-test, off the wire")
  Say("  (the canned frames are addressed to 192.168.1.50, so the")
  Say("   stack is configured to that address for this stage only)")

  Line("set mac        ", NetSetMac(#HW_LINK_WIRED, @npMac[0]))
  Line("set 192.168.1.50", NpCannedConfig())

  ; --- a real ARP request for us ---
  r = NetInput(#HW_LINK_WIRED, @npArp[0], 60)
  Line("ARP in  -> code", r)
  Line("  reply octets ", NetOutLen())
  If r <> 1
    WhyNet()
  EndIf
  If NetOutLen() = 60
    HexLine("  opcode (want 0002)", (PeekA(NetOutBuf() + 20) << 8) | PeekA(NetOutBuf() + 21), 4)
    MacLine("  reply goes to", NetOutBuf())
    MacLine("  and comes from", NetOutBuf() + 6)
    HexLine("  our IP in it ", (PeekA(NetOutBuf() + 28) << 24) | (PeekA(NetOutBuf() + 29) << 16) | (PeekA(NetOutBuf() + 30) << 8) | PeekA(NetOutBuf() + 31), 8)
    ; The pad. Eighteen zeros, and they must be zeros - see PADDING in
    ; net.pi4's header. A non-zero here is the board's own memory
    ; leaking onto the network.
    n = 0
    For i = 42 To 59
      If PeekA(NetOutBuf() + i) <> 0
        n = n + 1
      EndIf
    Next
    Line("  non-zero pad ", n)
  EndIf

  ; --- a real ICMP echo request ---
  ; Its two checksums are both correct in the canned bytes, so this
  ; grades verification in both directions at once: a library that skips
  ; the check and a library that computes it wrongly both fail here.
  r = NetInput(#HW_LINK_WIRED, @npEcho[0], 74)
  Line("echo in -> code", r)
  Line("  reply octets ", NetOutLen())
  If r <> 1
    WhyNet()
  EndIf
  If NetOutLen() = 74
    Line("  ICMP type (want 0)", PeekA(NetOutBuf() + 34))
    Line("  ICMP code (want 0)", PeekA(NetOutBuf() + 35))
    HexLine("  identifier (want 0001)", (PeekA(NetOutBuf() + 38) << 8) | PeekA(NetOutBuf() + 39), 4)
    HexLine("  sequence (want 0025) ", (PeekA(NetOutBuf() + 40) << 8) | PeekA(NetOutBuf() + 41), 4)
    Line("  TTL (want 64)", PeekA(NetOutBuf() + 22))
    HexLine("  ver/IHL (want 45)", PeekA(NetOutBuf() + 14), 2)
    ; The payload must come back unchanged. That is the whole contract
    ; of an echo, and it is what the pinging host matches on.
    n = 0
    For i = 0 To 31
      If PeekA(NetOutBuf() + 42 + i) <> npEcho[42 + i]
        n = n + 1
      EndIf
    Next
    Line("  payload octets changed (want 0)", n)
  EndIf

  ; --- the same echo with one payload octet flipped and the checksum
  ;     left alone. It must be REFUSED. ---
  npEcho[45] = npEcho[45] ! $01
  r = NetInput(#HW_LINK_WIRED, @npEcho[0], 74)
  Refusal("corrupt echo   ", r, #NET_E_ICMP_CKSUM)
  npEcho[45] = npEcho[45] ! $01
  PrintNl()

  ; ==================================================================
  ;  3. THE REAL CONFIGURATION
  ; ==================================================================
  Say("3. configuration")
  NetArpFlush(#HW_LINK_WIRED)
  ok = NetSetIPv4(#HW_LINK_WIRED, npIp, npMask, npGw)
  Line("set ipv4       ", ok)
  If ok = 0
    WhyNet()
    Say("=== stopping: the address, mask or gateway was refused ===")
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  MacLine("mac            ", @npMac[0])
  DotLine("address        ", NetIPv4(#HW_LINK_WIRED))
  DotLine("netmask        ", NetMask(#HW_LINK_WIRED))
  DotLine("gateway        ", NetGateway(#HW_LINK_WIRED))
  DotLine("subnet bcast   ", NetSubnetBroadcast(#HW_LINK_WIRED))
  DotLine("peer           ", npPeer)
  Line("configured     ", NetConfigured(#HW_LINK_WIRED))
  Line("udp bind       ", NetUdpBind(#HW_LINK_WIRED, #NP_UDP_PORT))
  Line("udp port       ", NetUdpBoundPort(#HW_LINK_WIRED))
  PrintNl()

  ; ==================================================================
  ;  4. BRING THE MAC UP
  ; ==================================================================
  ; After the protocol self-test, so that a failure here is unambiguous:
  ; everything above this line has already been proved.
  Say("4. bring-up")
  Line("set mac        ", GenetSetMac(@npMac[0]))
  Line("set rx region  ", GenetSetRxRegion(#NP_RX_REGION, #NP_RX_BYTES))
  ok = GenetStart(#NP_LINK_MS)
  Line("start ok       ", ok)
  If ok = 0
    WhyGenet()
    Say("=== stopping: the MAC is not up. The refusal suite still runs ===")
  EndIf
  If ok = 1
    Line("MAC-side link  ", GenetMacLinkStatus())
    HexLine("UMAC_CMD raw   ", GenetUmacCmd(), 8)
    HexLine("OOB raw        ", GenetOobCtrl(), 8)
  EndIf
  PrintNl()

  If ok = 1
    ; ================================================================
    ;  5. ARP FOR THE GATEWAY
    ; ================================================================
    ; The first thing that needs the network to answer. Two seconds, in
    ; eight clock-bounded slices.
    Say("5. ARP for the gateway")
    DotLine("asking for     ", npGw)
    r = NetArpRequest(#HW_LINK_WIRED, npGw)
    Line("built          ", r)
    Line("octets         ", NetOutLen())
    If r = 0
      WhyNet()
    Else
      sent = GenetSend(NetOutBuf(), NetOutLen(), #NP_TX_MS)
      Line("sent           ", sent)
      If sent = 0
        WhyGenet()
      EndIf
    EndIf
    got = 0
    i = 0
    While i < #NP_ANSWER_SLICES
      NpPump(#NP_ANSWER_SLICE_MS)
      If NetArpLookup(#HW_LINK_WIRED, npGw, @npPeerMac[0]) = 1
        got = 1
        Break
      EndIf
      i = i + 1
    Wend
    Line("slices used    ", i + 1)
    Line("resolved       ", got)
    If got = 1
      MacLine("gateway mac    ", @npPeerMac[0])
    Else
      Say("  the gateway did not answer. Stage 6 still works - it")
      Say("  answers what comes to it and needs nothing resolved.")
    EndIf
    Line("arp entries    ", NetArpCount(#HW_LINK_WIRED))
    PrintNl()

    ; ================================================================
    ;  6. THIRTY SECONDS OF ANSWERING - THE STAGE TO PING INTO
    ; ================================================================
    Say("6. serving. PING THE BOARD NOW:")
    UartWriteStr("     ping ")
    PrintDec((npIp >> 24) & $FF)
    UartWrite(46)
    PrintDec((npIp >> 16) & $FF)
    UartWrite(46)
    PrintDec((npIp >> 8) & $FF)
    UartWrite(46)
    PrintDec(npIp & $FF)
    PrintNl()
    Say("  thirty seconds, in sixty half-second slices. A DURATION,")
    Say("  not a poll count - see GenetRecvWait in genet.pi4.")
    npFramesIn = 0
    npRepliesOut = 0
    npArpAnswered = 0
    npPingAnswered = 0
    npUdpIn = 0
    npSendFail = 0
    i = 0
    While i < #NP_SERVE_SLICES
      NpPump(#NP_SERVE_SLICE_MS)
      i = i + 1
    Wend
    Line("frames in      ", npFramesIn)
    Line("replies out    ", npRepliesOut)
    Line("arp answered   ", npArpAnswered)
    Line("ping answered  ", npPingAnswered)
    Line("udp received   ", npUdpIn)
    Line("send failures  ", npSendFail)
    Line("net ignored    ", NetDropCount())
    Line("genet rx count ", GenetRxCount())
    Line("genet tx count ", GenetTxCount())
    Line("arp entries    ", NetArpCount(#HW_LINK_WIRED))
    If npPingAnswered > 0
      Say("  >>> THE BOARD ANSWERED A PING. <<<")
    EndIf
    PrintNl()

    ; ================================================================
    ;  7. OUTBOUND - A PING AND A DATAGRAM
    ; ================================================================
    Say("7. outbound")
    Line("peer resolved  ", NpEnsurePeer())

    got = 0
    i = 1
    While i <= 4
      ; RE-RESOLVE BEFORE EVERY BUILD, not once before the loop. The
      ; loop runs for seconds and an ARP entry has a lifetime; asking
      ; once at the top and trusting the answer four pings later is
      ; what produced the contradiction of 2026-08-26. NpEnsurePeer is
      ; free when the address is already known.
      If NpEnsurePeer() = 0
        Line("peer lost      ", i)
        Say("  the peer stopped answering ARP part way through the loop.")
        Break
      EndIf
      r = NetPingBuild(#HW_LINK_WIRED, npPeer, $4321, i, 32)
      If r = 0
        Line("ping build     ", r)
        WhyNet()
        If NetError() = #NET_E_NO_ARP
          Say("  THE LINE ABOVE CONTRADICTS THE LINE BEFORE IT. The address")
          Say("  was resolved microseconds ago and is unknown now. That is")
          Say("  an internal inconsistency in net.pi4, not a network fault:")
          Say("  report this whole stage rather than re-cabling anything.")
        EndIf
        Break
      EndIf
      If GenetSend(NetOutBuf(), NetOutLen(), #NP_TX_MS) = 0
        WhyGenet()
        Break
      EndIf
      n = 0
      While n < 4
        If NpPump(250) = 3
          got = got + 1
          Break
        EndIf
        n = n + 1
      Wend
      i = i + 1
    Wend
    Line("pings sent     ", i - 1)
    Line("replies        ", got)
    If got > 0
      DotLine("last reply from", NetPongFrom())
      Line("last identifier", NetPongIdent())
      Line("last sequence  ", NetPongSeq())
      Line("payload octets ", NetPongBytes())
    EndIf

    i = 0
    While i < 32
      npUdpOut[i] = 97 + (i % 23)
      i = i + 1
    Wend
    If NpEnsurePeer() = 0
      Line("udp built      ", 0)
      Say("  not attempted: the peer's hardware address is not known.")
      r = 0
    Else
      r = NetUdpBuild(#HW_LINK_WIRED, npPeer, #NP_UDP_PORT, 5555, @npUdpOut[0], 32)
      Line("udp built      ", r)
    EndIf
    If r = 0
      WhyNet()
    Else
      Line("udp octets     ", NetOutLen())
      Line("udp sent       ", GenetSend(NetOutBuf(), NetOutLen(), #NP_TX_MS))
    EndIf
    PrintNl()
  EndIf

  ; ==================================================================
  ;  8. THE REFUSALS
  ; ==================================================================
  ; Every documented refusal in net.pi4, provoked deliberately. A
  ; refusal that stops firing is a refusal somebody deleted, and this is
  ; the only place that would notice.
  ;
  ; IT RUNS LAST AND IT LEAVES THE STACK UNCONFIGURED. NetReset() is the
  ; only way to get back to "no MAC address has been set", so nothing
  ; that needs the network can come after this.
  Say("8. the refusals")

  ; STAGE 8 ESTABLISHES ITS OWN PRECONDITIONS, and until 2026-08-26 it
  ; did not. It replays stage 2's canned frames and it builds to
  ; addresses chosen for stage 2's canned subnet - but by the time it
  ; runs, stage 3 has reconfigured the stack to the real bench address
  ; and stages 6 and 7 have filled the ARP cache. Two of its cases
  ; therefore reported the wrong answer, and BOTH were this file's
  ; fault rather than net.pi4's:
  ;
  ;   "bad ICMP cksum" wanted -112 and got 0. npEcho is addressed to
  ;   192.168.1.50 and the board was 169.254.183.50, so net_RecvIp's
  ;   destination filter returned #NET_IN_IGNORED before the ICMP
  ;   checksum was ever computed. Every OTHER npEcho case in this stage
  ;   kept working only because each provokes a HEADER check - version,
  ;   IHL, header checksum, fragment, total length - and every one of
  ;   those runs BEFORE the destination filter. This is the only case in
  ;   the stage that has to get past it, so it is the only one that
  ;   broke, which is exactly why the failure looked inexplicable.
  ;
  ;   "unresolved host" wanted -118 and got 0. It builds to
  ;   192.168.1.201, which is OFF-LINK under 169.254.0.0/16, so
  ;   NetRouteMac resolved THE GATEWAY - cached by then - and the build
  ;   succeeded. The case was never testing an unresolved host at all.
  ;   Under the canned /24 that address is on-link, and the flush below
  ;   guarantees it is unknown.
  ;
  ; The general rule, and the reason this is written out at length: a
  ; stage that consumes fixtures must configure the machine those
  ; fixtures were made for. Inheriting whatever the previous stage left
  ; behind is how a suite starts grading a different question from the
  ; one its labels claim.
  NetArpFlush(#HW_LINK_WIRED)
  Line("canned config  ", NpCannedConfig())

  ; --- things that need no configuration at all ---
  NetInput(#HW_LINK_WIRED, 0, 60)
  Refusal("null frame     ", NetError(), #NET_E_ARG)

  r = NetInput(#HW_LINK_WIRED, @npEcho[0], 13)
  Refusal("13-byte frame  ", r, #NET_E_SHORT)

  ; --- malformed IPv4, each field broken one at a time ---
  npEcho[14] = $65                                   ; version 6, IHL 5
  r = NetInput(#HW_LINK_WIRED, @npEcho[0], 74)
  Refusal("version 6      ", r, #NET_E_IP_VERSION)
  npEcho[14] = $44                                   ; version 4, IHL 4
  r = NetInput(#HW_LINK_WIRED, @npEcho[0], 74)
  Refusal("IHL 4          ", r, #NET_E_IP_HEADER)
  npEcho[14] = $45

  npEcho[24] = npEcho[24] ! $01                      ; header checksum
  r = NetInput(#HW_LINK_WIRED, @npEcho[0], 74)
  Refusal("bad IP cksum   ", r, #NET_E_IP_CKSUM)
  npEcho[24] = npEcho[24] ! $01

  npEcho[20] = $60                                   ; DF and More Fragments
  r = NetInput(#HW_LINK_WIRED, @npEcho[0], 74)
  Refusal("fragment (MF)  ", r, #NET_E_FRAGMENT)
  npEcho[20] = $40
  npEcho[21] = $01                                   ; a fragment offset
  r = NetInput(#HW_LINK_WIRED, @npEcho[0], 74)
  Refusal("fragment (off) ", r, #NET_E_FRAGMENT)
  npEcho[21] = $00

  npEcho[17] = $FF                                   ; total length 255
  r = NetInput(#HW_LINK_WIRED, @npEcho[0], 74)
  Refusal("total too long ", r, #NET_E_IP_LENGTH)
  npEcho[17] = $3C

  npEcho[36] = npEcho[36] ! $01                      ; ICMP checksum
  r = NetInput(#HW_LINK_WIRED, @npEcho[0], 74)
  Refusal("bad ICMP cksum ", r, #NET_E_ICMP_CKSUM)
  npEcho[36] = npEcho[36] ! $01

  ; --- ARP ---
  r = NetInput(#HW_LINK_WIRED, @npArp[0], 20)
  Refusal("short ARP      ", r, #NET_E_ARP_SHORT)
  npArp[15] = $02                                    ; hardware type 2
  r = NetInput(#HW_LINK_WIRED, @npArp[0], 60)
  Refusal("ARP not ethernet", r, #NET_E_ARP_PROTO)
  npArp[15] = $01

  ; --- receiving into our own staging buffer ---
  r = NetInput(#HW_LINK_WIRED, NetOutBuf(), 60)
  Refusal("rx into out buf", r, #NET_E_ARG)

  ; --- builders ---
  r = NetUdpBuild(#HW_LINK_WIRED, NetMakeIPv4(192, 168, 1, 201), 7777, 5555, @npUdpOut[0], 32)
  Refusal("unresolved host", NetError(), #NET_E_NO_ARP)
  NetUdpBuild(#HW_LINK_WIRED, npPeer, 7777, 5555, @npUdpOut[0], 1473)
  Refusal("payload 1473   ", NetError(), #NET_E_TOO_BIG)
  NetUdpBuild(#HW_LINK_WIRED, npPeer, 0, 5555, @npUdpOut[0], 4)
  Refusal("port 0         ", NetError(), #NET_E_PORT)
  NetPingBuild(#HW_LINK_WIRED, npPeer, 1, 1, 1473)
  Refusal("ping 1473      ", NetError(), #NET_E_TOO_BIG)

  ; --- configuration ---
  NetSetIPv4(#HW_LINK_WIRED, npIp, NetMakeIPv4(255, 0, 255, 0), 0)
  Refusal("mask 255.0.255.0", NetError(), #NET_E_MASK)
  NetSetIPv4(#HW_LINK_WIRED, npIp, npMask, NetMakeIPv4(10, 0, 0, 1))
  Refusal("gateway off-net", NetError(), #NET_E_GATEWAY)
  NetSetMac(#HW_LINK_WIRED, @npArp[0])                               ; six $FF - a group address
  Refusal("broadcast mac  ", NetError(), #NET_E_ARG)

  ; --- off-link with no gateway. Reconfigure without one first. ---
  NetSetIPv4(#HW_LINK_WIRED, npIp, npMask, 0)
  NetUdpBuild(#HW_LINK_WIRED, NetMakeIPv4(8, 8, 8, 8), 53, 5555, @npUdpOut[0], 4)
  Refusal("no gateway     ", NetError(), #NET_E_NO_ROUTE)

  ; --- and finally, nothing configured at all ---
  NetReset()
  r = NetInput(#HW_LINK_WIRED, @npArp[0], 60)
  Refusal("no mac, input  ", r, #NET_E_NO_MAC)
  NetArpRequest(#HW_LINK_WIRED, npGw)
  Refusal("no mac, arp    ", NetError(), #NET_E_NO_MAC)
  NetSetMac(#HW_LINK_WIRED, @npMac[0])
  NetArpRequest(#HW_LINK_WIRED, npGw)
  Refusal("no ip, arp     ", NetError(), #NET_E_NO_IP)
  PrintNl()

  Say("=== done. The stack is left UNCONFIGURED by stage 8. ===")
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure
